Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below is a “thinking sheet” that 

• lists the columns that are likely to matter for counting overtakes, together with the way they could be used, and  
• shows ten exploratory SQLite queries – from very simple look‑ups to a reasonably complete (yet still readable) over‑take counter.  
All queries are only meant to let you examine / verify the data; therefore every SELECT finishes with LIMIT 20 (the counter queries also keep the LIMIT so that they only print a few sample rows).

────────────────────────────────────────
1.  Relevant columns and their possible usage
────────────────────────────────────────
races_ext
    race_id           : primary key to join all race related tables  
    year, round       : season context (optional)  
    is_pit_data_available : =1 → race has full lap‑by‑lap & pit information (condition)  

lap_positions
    race_id, driver_id, lap : composite key  
    position          : car’s position at the *end* of that lap / phase  
    lap_type          : values such as  
                        • 'Starting Position – Grid Drop'  
                        • 'Race'  
                        • 'Pit In', 'Pit Out', ‘Safety‑Car’, …  
                        Used to classify the *kind* of over‑take (start, on‑track, pit‑cycle, etc.)  

pit_stops
    race_id, driver_id, lap : lap on which the car enters the pit lane – needed to recognise pit‑in/pit‑out position changes  
    stop              : 1st, 2nd, … pit stop of that driver in the race  

retirements
    race_id, driver_id, lap : retirement lap → the driver disappears from the running order afterwards  
    status_id, retirement_type : reason (accident, mechanical, …).  When somebody retires, every other driver may gain a place without an “overtake” – must be excluded.

results
    race_id, driver_id, position_order : final positions – handy for sanity checks  

lap_times / lap_times_ext  (optional)
    Not strictly required; lap_positions already carries the ranking.

status                        (optional)
    status_id, status : decode the status_id in retirements.

────────────────────────────────────────
2.  Exploratory SQL queries
────────────────────────────────────────
(The queries are deliberately limited to 20 rows each, so they are safe to run in every SQLite front‑end.)

```sql
-- 1. List the races that actually contain pit‑stop / lap‑position data
SELECT race_id,
       year,
       round,
       short_name,
       name
FROM   races_ext
WHERE  is_pit_data_available = 1
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Which “lap_type” values exist?
SELECT DISTINCT lap_type
FROM   lap_positions
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Sample of lap‑by‑lap positions for the first race with pit data
SELECT lp.*
FROM   lap_positions AS lp
WHERE  lp.race_id = (SELECT race_id
                     FROM   races_ext
                     WHERE  is_pit_data_available = 1
                     ORDER  BY race_id
                     LIMIT  1)
ORDER  BY lap, position
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Pit‑stop log for the same sample race
SELECT *
FROM   pit_stops
WHERE  race_id = (SELECT race_id
                  FROM   races_ext
                  WHERE  is_pit_data_available = 1
                  ORDER  BY race_id
                  LIMIT  1)
ORDER  BY driver_id, stop
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Retirements inside that race
SELECT r.*, s.status
FROM   retirements AS r
LEFT   JOIN status AS s USING (status_id)
WHERE  r.race_id = (SELECT race_id
                    FROM   races_ext
                    WHERE  is_pit_data_available = 1
                    ORDER  BY race_id
                    LIMIT  1)
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Check how a driver’s position changes between consecutive laps
--    (example: driver 1 in the sample race)
SELECT cur.lap,
       prev.position AS pos_prev,
       cur.position AS pos_cur
FROM   lap_positions AS cur
JOIN   lap_positions AS prev
       ON  prev.race_id  = cur.race_id
       AND prev.driver_id = cur.driver_id
       AND prev.lap       = cur.lap - 1
WHERE  cur.race_id  = (SELECT race_id
                       FROM   races_ext
                       WHERE  is_pit_data_available = 1
                       ORDER  BY race_id
                       LIMIT  1)
  AND  cur.driver_id = 1
ORDER  BY cur.lap
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 7. “Who is directly in front of whom” on every lap (sample race)
SELECT cur.lap,
       cur.driver_id        AS follower,
       lead.driver_id       AS leader,
       cur.position         AS follower_pos,
       lead.position        AS leader_pos,
       cur.lap_type
FROM   lap_positions AS cur
JOIN   lap_positions AS lead
       ON  lead.race_id = cur.race_id
       AND lead.lap     = cur.lap
       AND lead.position = cur.position - 1   -- car right in front
WHERE  cur.race_id = (SELECT race_id
                      FROM   races_ext
                      WHERE  is_pit_data_available = 1
                      ORDER  BY race_id
                      LIMIT  1)
ORDER  BY cur.lap, cur.position
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Detect NEW “following relationships”
--    (driver was NOT behind 'leader' on the previous lap, but IS now)
SELECT cur.race_id,
       cur.lap,
       cur.driver_id  AS follower,
       lead.driver_id AS leader,
       cur.lap_type
FROM   lap_positions AS cur
JOIN   lap_positions AS lead                          -- car ahead on current lap
       ON  lead.race_id = cur.race_id
       AND lead.lap     = cur.lap
       AND lead.position = cur.position - 1
LEFT  JOIN lap_positions AS prev_follower             -- same follower on previous lap
       ON  prev_follower.race_id  = cur.race_id
       AND prev_follower.driver_id = cur.driver_id
       AND prev_follower.lap       = cur.lap - 1
LEFT  JOIN lap_positions AS prev_leader               -- car that was ahead of the follower on previous lap
       ON  prev_leader.race_id = prev_follower.race_id
       AND prev_leader.lap     = prev_follower.lap
       AND prev_leader.position = prev_follower.position - 1
WHERE  cur.race_id = (SELECT race_id
                      FROM   races_ext
                      WHERE  is_pit_data_available = 1
                      ORDER  BY race_id
                      LIMIT  1)
  AND  prev_follower.driver_id IS NOT NULL            -- make sure we have a previous lap
  AND  (prev_leader.driver_id IS NULL                 -- follower had *no* car in front last lap (start / pit‑out)
        OR prev_leader.driver_id <> lead.driver_id)   -- or the leader was a different car
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Add PIT and RETIREMENT filters to turn the above
--    into “on‑track” overtakes only (sample race)
WITH running AS (  -- drivers that are still in the race at the current lap
      SELECT lp.race_id,
             lp.driver_id,
             lp.lap
      FROM   lap_positions lp
      LEFT  JOIN retirements r
             ON  r.race_id = lp.race_id
             AND r.driver_id = lp.driver_id
             AND r.lap      <= lp.lap
      WHERE  r.driver_id IS NULL       -- driver not retired *yet*
)
SELECT cur.race_id,
       cur.lap,
       cur.driver_id  AS follower,
       lead.driver_id AS leader,
       CASE
         WHEN cur.lap         = 1 THEN 'Race Start'
         WHEN prev_follower.lap_type LIKE 'Pit%' THEN 'Pit Exit'
         WHEN cur.lap_type  LIKE 'Pit%' THEN 'Pit Entry'
         ELSE 'On‑Track'
       END                         AS overtake_type
FROM   lap_positions      AS cur
JOIN   running            AS run_cur  ON run_cur.race_id = cur.race_id
                                       AND run_cur.driver_id = cur.driver_id
                                       AND run_cur.lap = cur.lap
JOIN   lap_positions      AS lead
       ON  lead.race_id = cur.race_id
       AND lead.lap     = cur.lap
       AND lead.position = cur.position - 1
JOIN   running            AS run_lead ON run_lead.race_id = lead.race_id
                                       AND run_lead.driver_id = lead.driver_id
                                       AND run_lead.lap = lead.lap
LEFT  JOIN lap_positions  AS prev_follower
       ON  prev_follower.race_id  = cur.race_id
       AND prev_follower.driver_id = cur.driver_id
       AND prev_follower.lap       = cur.lap - 1
LEFT  JOIN lap_positions  AS prev_lead
       ON  prev_lead.race_id = prev_follower.race_id
       AND prev_lead.lap     = prev_follower.lap
       AND prev_lead.position = prev_follower.position - 1
WHERE  cur.race_id = (SELECT race_id
                      FROM   races_ext
                      WHERE  is_pit_data_available = 1
                      ORDER  BY race_id
                      LIMIT  1)
  AND  prev_follower.driver_id IS NOT NULL
  AND  (prev_lead.driver_id IS NULL
        OR prev_lead.driver_id <> lead.driver_id)
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Grand‑total: how many times has each over‑take type happened
--     in *all* races that have pit data?
WITH races_with_pits AS (
     SELECT race_id
     FROM   races_ext
     WHERE  is_pit_data_available = 1
),
running AS (
     SELECT lp.race_id,
            lp.driver_id,
            lp.lap
     FROM   lap_positions lp
     JOIN   races_with_pits rw USING (race_id)
     LEFT  JOIN retirements r
            ON  r.race_id  = lp.race_id
            AND r.driver_id = lp.driver_id
            AND r.lap      <= lp.lap
     WHERE  r.driver_id IS NULL
),
overtakes AS (
     SELECT cur.race_id,
            cur.lap,
            cur.driver_id  AS follower,
            lead.driver_id AS leader,
            CASE
              WHEN cur.lap         = 1 THEN 'Race Start'
              WHEN prev_follower.lap_type LIKE 'Pit%' THEN 'Pit Exit'
              WHEN cur.lap_type  LIKE 'Pit%' THEN 'Pit Entry'
              ELSE 'On‑Track'
            END AS overtake_type
     FROM   lap_positions      AS cur
     JOIN   races_with_pits    rw         ON rw.race_id = cur.race_id
     JOIN   running            run_cur    ON run_cur.race_id = cur.race_id
                                          AND run_cur.driver_id = cur.driver_id
                                          AND run_cur.lap = cur.lap
     JOIN   lap_positions      AS lead
            ON  lead.race_id = cur.race_id
            AND lead.lap     = cur.lap
            AND lead.position = cur.position - 1
     JOIN   running            run_lead   ON run_lead.race_id = lead.race_id
                                          AND run_lead.driver_id = lead.driver_id
                                          AND run_lead.lap = lead.lap
     LEFT  JOIN lap_positions  AS prev_follower
            ON  prev_follower.race_id  = cur.race_id
            AND prev_follower.driver_id = cur.driver_id
            AND prev_follower.lap       = cur.lap - 1
     LEFT  JOIN lap_positions  AS prev_lead
            ON  prev_lead.race_id = prev_follower.race_id
            AND prev_lead.lap     = prev_follower.lap
            AND prev_lead.position = prev_follower.position - 1
     WHERE  prev_follower.driver_id IS NOT NULL
       AND  (prev_lead.driver_id IS NULL
             OR prev_lead.driver_id <> lead.driver_id)
)
SELECT overtake_type,
       COUNT(*) AS number_of_overtakes
FROM   overtakes
GROUP  BY overtake_type
ORDER  BY number_of_overtakes DESC
LIMIT  20;
```

The last statement gives you the requested figure – “how many times has each type of over‑take occurred in Formula 1 (for races with full pit‑stop data)” – broken down into:

• Race Start overtakes  
• On‑Track overtakes (pure, wheel‑to‑wheel)  
• Pit Entry position changes  
• Pit Exit position changes  

If you remove the final LIMIT 20 you’ll see the complete summary.
